From 84840488d5b79f62bca9fc34bc6314a5d3d582d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeanluca <jeanlucafp@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 10:41:49 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix(persistence): save the equipment-free base score instead
 of rebuilding it

A player's character showed STR/INT -88. The database held str=int=-88
with no attribute bonus on any equipped item: exactly 100 below the 12
the character was created with, on all four attributes (dex/con too).

The character table stores the CurrentScore (base + equipment) and the
login rebuilt the base as stored - equipment bonus, computed with the
rules of that moment (deriveBaseScore). Any change to those rules between
a save and a login moves the base for good. The refine scaling of
2026-08-17 (51574996) turned a +9 Pedra Amunra from +100 into +200 per
attribute: a character saved with it at +100 came back with a base 100
lower on every attribute, and kept it after the stone came off. Item
catalog edits, conditional bonuses and expiring gear do the same.

Migration 0027 adds base_str/int/dex/con and base_max_hp/mp. The world
always saves the base it simulates; the login takes it as saved and the
existing refreshScore rebuilds the CurrentScore with today's rules, so a
rule change can move the CurrentScore but never the base. A row saved
before 0027 (NULL base) still derives once and the next save stores it.
A save that does not carry the base (a tmServer older than this change,
e.g. after a rollback) clears it, so a stale base is never trusted; both
deploy orders are safe. A negative derived base is logged.

Data is not repaired here: the test server is in alpha and players know.

Tests:
- TestBaseScoreSurvivesItemRuleChange: the reported case (saved at +100,
  loaded at +200), a second rule change and the stone coming off; it
  shows 112 instead of 212 without the fix;
- TestBaseScoreLegacyRowDerivesOnceThenPersists;
- proto mapping in dbclient and dbserver;
- TestBaseScoreColumns (integration, Postgres 16): round trip, NULL for a
  pre-0027 row, and the clear-on-save-without-base rule.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
